#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888} :activity/id #uuid "0ac63a17-8eba-58fd-81f6-c5dfcfca821d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839287e3-b143-5415-99de-08e22d1fe7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac8fd50-dcb6-5b86-b402-c970434163b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197974} {:answer/id #uuid "fca362ab-06c7-5e0f-a0ea-d44fa5a42449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197975} {:answer/id #uuid "401f21c4-4ada-5f5e-b68f-b4edd51e55ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197976} {:answer/id #uuid "4d850692-8440-57f8-a931-14cb51d6f903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b076631-de18-587b-978a-08fc530f03da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab0253d0-1db9-532d-bb4a-604c88a16da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98103" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 18.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(2,2).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=18.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=18\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(3,3),\\,(-3,-3),\\,(2,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(3,3)$ | $(-3,-3)$ | $(2,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $42$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-3,-3),$ and its maximum value is $42.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446746} {:answer/id #uuid "42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446755} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446759} {:answer/id #uuid "8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446773} {:answer/id #uuid "980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162800" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364650} {:answer/id #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364651} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364652} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364653} {:answer/id #uuid "42cfec62-7180-5eb9-8dbb-e33d7fe7ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" :question/math-academy-id "q-99230" :question/problem "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361102} {:answer/id #uuid "838bc3f9-5547-5aae-9ce5-c937af91a9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} {:answer/id #uuid "83aec126-3e7b-519c-90b5-432b36990afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361422} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4af1fe-8cbf-5c53-aeb4-2b02ab93e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" :question/math-academy-id "q-99163" :question/problem "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0499cbca-daad-57e1-9934-25522e310898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278629} {:answer/id #uuid "08288e31-44a4-5cbd-b685-8435569179d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} {:answer/id #uuid "10043f84-5b2d-5986-8b89-d3b961ecb64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278735} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71714-856d-5694-afb1-6a391106f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278} {:answer/id #uuid "fea569a7-1a86-5d69-9043-62f12b437f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162780" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y &=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$"} :step/id #uuid "f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545183}]}
